<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The same mark for a themed icon, in one colour.

  Android 13 tints this layer with the user's wallpaper palette, so the two-colour version cannot be
  reused: the accent would be repainted anyway, and the bar drawn in the same ink as the magnet still
  reads, because it crosses the poles rather than sharing an edge with them.

  The name is the brief. A horseshoe magnet stands with its poles down, and the way through is laid
  across them: the magnet and the gate are the same object, and the bar is the part that opens. Nothing
  reaches wider than the magnet itself, which is what keeps the mark from reading as a piece of furniture
  - the first version did, and at 48px that was all anyone would have seen.

  Geometry is shared with the mark drawn in the app's header (AppUi.ProductMark): one drawing, two
  places. The strokes are heavy for their canvas on purpose - at 48px in a launcher, or 30dp beside a
  title, a thin arch turns to mush and the cut that carries the meaning disappears first.

  Drawn to the adaptive-icon safe zone: everything sits inside the central 66 of 108, so no launcher mask
  - circle, squircle, teardrop - can clip any of it. Two colours only: the gate in the app's off-white,
  the path in the accent it spends on the control you came to press, so the icon and the screen behind it
  are plainly the same product.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

  <!-- the magnet, standing as a gate -->
  <path
      android:pathData="M30,80 L30,52 A24,24 0 0 1 78,52 L78,80"
      android:strokeColor="#000000"
      android:strokeWidth="11"
      android:strokeLineCap="round"
      android:strokeLineJoin="round" />

  <!-- the way through, laid across its poles and never wider than they are -->
  <path
      android:pathData="M30,66 L78,66"
      android:strokeColor="#000000"
      android:strokeWidth="9"
      android:strokeLineCap="round" />
</vector>
